WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.560
Think it's all just CGI and production tricks&nbsp;
that bring your favorite superheroes to life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.360
on screen? From damaged vocal chords to&nbsp;
broken bones to an actual death on set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.811
you won't believe how dangerous&nbsp;
playing a superhero can actually be.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.540
Jaimie Alexander, who plays the warrior Lady Sif&nbsp;
in the "Thor" franchise, suffered various injuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.500
while filming "Thor: The Dark World." Luckily,&nbsp;
the film's production could work around her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.740
giving the actor time to recuperate without&nbsp;
delaying the production of the movie.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.640
Alexander was an athlete in her youth,&nbsp;
so she carried that physical prowess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.619
with her into her acting career and has&nbsp;
always been willing to do her own stunts.

00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:35.779
"I've got this completely under control!"

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.860
Describing the injuries she suffered on the set of&nbsp;
"Thor: The Dark World," Alexander told Page Six:

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.300
"It was raining, it was dark outside,&nbsp;
it was like 5 in the morning — I went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.540
down a metal staircase and slipped&nbsp;
and slipped a disc in my thoracic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.500
spine and chipped 11 of my vertebrae.&nbsp;
I knocked my left shoulder out of place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.100
and tore my rhomboid on my right side...&nbsp;
It took me out of filming for a month."

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.480
These severe injuries didn't stop Alexander&nbsp;
from reprising her role in the "Thor" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.860
nor did they keep her from taking on another&nbsp;
stunt-intensive role when she agreed to play Jane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.900
Doe in "Blindspot." She also sustained numerous&nbsp;
injuries from her stunt work on that series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.539
Despite the pain, she loves doing her own stunts&nbsp;
and takes pride in her physical endurance.

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:19.080
Noah Centineo has enjoyed major success in&nbsp;
recent years, thanks to his lead role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.400
Netflix's "The Recruit" and his performance&nbsp;
as Atom Smasher in "Black Adam." But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.240
triumph comes with costs. Centineo told&nbsp;
Jimmy Fallon on "The Tonight Show" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.780
he suffered a pretty painful injury while&nbsp;
shooting a scene as Atom Smasher under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.680
the sweltering Atlanta sun. He explained&nbsp;
that he was trying to add some flare to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.034
the scene by pretending to hit his knee&nbsp;
on a parked car while he was running.

00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:43.854
"And I spin around like 'ahhhh,' like that&nbsp;
hurt. And my arm goes up and then dislocates."

00:01:47.293 --> 00:01:52.200
Medics struggled to get Centineo's shoulder back&nbsp;
in its socket, due to his tight costume. Filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.820
was paused as they cut the sleeve open. After&nbsp;
13 tries, they finally popped his shoulder back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.400
in — only for Centineo to immediately pop it&nbsp;
back out by lifting his arm to cheer. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.420
his shoulder was easier to fix the second&nbsp;
time. Although it's a fairly minor injury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.560
in the grand scheme of things, production ground&nbsp;
to a halt while the medics dealt with Centineo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.939
and the wardrobe department surely&nbsp;
had to replace his damaged suit.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:16.980
Robert Downey Jr. might seem invincible on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.660
but he actually broke his ankle while&nbsp;
filming a stunt for "Iron Man 3." Guy Pearce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.840
who played Aldrich Killian in the&nbsp;
film, revealed further details to GQ.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.260
"Robert broke his ankle in the middle of&nbsp;
that film, 'cause he had to do a stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.120
where he had to jump from one platform down&nbsp;
to another platform and be on a cable. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.480
wanted to rehearse it, and he said, 'No, I&nbsp;
don't need to rehearse it,' and he jumped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.660
and the guy holding the cable wasn't&nbsp;
ready or something, and he landed hard."

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.520
Interestingly enough, the take where Downey&nbsp;
broke his ankle actually made it into the movie.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.440
After the film was done, Downey told media outlets&nbsp;
his body held up fine, and joked that the delay in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.159
production gave them an opportunity to work on the&nbsp;
script and make "Iron Man 3" into a better movie.

00:02:54.159 --> 00:02:55.800
"I did alright. I broke my ankle once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.387
but then that gave us time to&nbsp;
do some more script provisions."

00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:05.340
Believe it or not, Chris Evans almost turned&nbsp;
the iconic role of Captain America down. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.060
once he was on board, Evans embraced the&nbsp;
role's more physical aspects, even doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.560
some of his own stunts in "Captain America:&nbsp;
The First Avenger." The actor told MTV News:

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.820
"The good thing about Cap is that most of his&nbsp;
abilities are rooted in what a human being is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.500
capable of — he's not flying, he's not shooting&nbsp;
fire. For the most part, the stunts involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.700
were hand-to-hand combat, so any time I can put&nbsp;
the gloves on and get in the ring, I'll do it."

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.180
Unfortunately, Evans injured his&nbsp;
arm while filming "Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.320
Civil War." In a pivotal scene, Steve&nbsp;
Rogers tries to stop a helicopter from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.759
taking off with his bare hands.&nbsp;
Evans told Entertainment Tonight:

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:40.920
"That was in the first week of filming.&nbsp;
And I actually messed up my arm doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.520
I really did. It's a fake helicopter,&nbsp;
obviously, but it was drifting beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.780
the ability of control. I tweaked a little&nbsp;
something. To this day, I'm still messed up."

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.580
Notably, Robert Downey Jr. wasn't surprised&nbsp;
by this injury. When he arrived at the "Civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.540
War" set and saw what Evans had been filming,&nbsp;
the actor immediately knew that this shot was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.619
risky. Luckily, after Evans sustained&nbsp;
his injury, Downey was there to help.

00:04:01.935 --> 00:04:05.880
Hugh Jackman has suffered a litany of&nbsp;
injuries playing Wolverine over the years.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.320
During the production of "X-Men," Jackman&nbsp;
smashed his ear and in a separate instance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.359
injured his groin after getting caught in&nbsp;
a stunt harness. The actor told Yahoo!:

00:04:14.359 --> 00:04:16.380
"That was pretty brutal. I screamed so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.680
and they kept going because they&nbsp;
figured it was me primal-yell emoting."

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.540
Then, during the production of "X2: X-Men&nbsp;
United," Jackman discovered just how dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.400
Wolverine's metal claws truly are when he&nbsp;
cut the skin next to his eye — which left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.200
a scar — and his upper thigh. Jackman talked&nbsp;
about the latter injury on "The Graham Norton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.920
Show," explaining that it happened while&nbsp;
filming a nude scene. He turned a corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.920
saw a group of female crew members&nbsp;
who had gathered together as a joke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.640
to surprise and embarrass him, and&nbsp;
reflexively tried to cover himself.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.192
"And of course the first I did was like —"

00:04:50.192 --> 00:04:52.625
"I did cut myself on the inner thigh."

00:04:52.845 --> 00:04:55.740
External injuries aren't the&nbsp;
only type Jackman experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.160
while playing Wolverine. During an&nbsp;
interview with the BBC, he admitted:

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.120
"I've done some damage to my voice with&nbsp;
Wolverine. My falsetto is not as strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.080
as it used to be and that I directly put&nbsp;
down to some of the growling and yelling. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.500
vocal teacher in drama school would've been&nbsp;
horrified with some of the things I did."

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.340
Jason Momoa suffered multiple&nbsp;
injuries while filming "Aquaman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.700
and the Lost Kingdom," telling Ellen&nbsp;
DeGeneres that he scratched his cornea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.940
during production — and that's&nbsp;
just for starters. He elaborated:

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.200
"I messed up my eyes, just got something in&nbsp;
it that kind of cut it up. And I gotta get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.260
surgery on a hernia. I got ribs&nbsp;
out. I'm just getting beat up."

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.160
Momoa assured DeGeneres the injuries&nbsp;
were well worth it, however, saying:

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.320
"It's gonna be a great&nbsp;
movie. You're gonna love it."

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.920
DeGeneres playfully gave Momoa a&nbsp;
hard time for getting injured in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.340
every movie he seems to make. Momoa&nbsp;
chalked it up to his advancing age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.033
and passion for filmmaking. It's rough&nbsp;
stuff, but he considers it worth it.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.480
Like many movies affected&nbsp;
by the COVID-19 pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.080
"The Batman" saw its fair share of&nbsp;
difficulties during production. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.740
of them even predated the pandemic.&nbsp;
Star Robert Pattinson told GQ:

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.180
"I broke my wrist at the beginning of it&nbsp;
all, doing a stunt, even before COVID. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.200
the whole first section was trying to keep&nbsp;
working out — looking like a penguin. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.600
remember when that seemed like the&nbsp;
worst thing that could go wrong."

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.160
Soon the film was shut down because of the&nbsp;
pandemic, giving Pattinson plenty of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.520
to nurse his broken wrist and quarantine&nbsp;
with the rest of the world. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.960
after the movie finally went back into production&nbsp;
with new regulations, Pattinson tested positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.220
and production was paused again. "The Batman"&nbsp;
shoot ultimately encompassed a staggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.000
18 months, leaving the star feeling&nbsp;
beleaguered and burned out. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.840
breaking his wrist during a stunt turned out&nbsp;
to be one of the smaller challenges Pattinson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.220
faced while bringing this new iteration of the&nbsp;
Caped Crusader to life during a global pandemic.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.320
Jeremy Renner was in the news in early&nbsp;
2023 after getting injured saving his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.920
nephew from being crushed. But this&nbsp;
real-life hero has also made a name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.240
for himself by doing his own stunt work, and&nbsp;
sometimes, these stunts result in injuries.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.218
Renner discussed his commitment to&nbsp;
doing his own stunts with Howard Stern:

00:06:52.218 --> 00:06:55.320
"You don't want them to be&nbsp;
pulled out of the movie by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.699
some guy with a bad wig trying to do the stunt."

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:03.000
This incurs a major price tag, however:&nbsp;
Renner broke both his arms while filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.900
the action comedy "Tag." Astonishingly,&nbsp;
he went back to work the same day.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.920
Renner has gotten beaten up while playing&nbsp;
Hawkeye too. While filming a stunt for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.860
"The Avengers," the actor tore a&nbsp;
muscle in his back and had to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.340
a break from filming. Director Joss&nbsp;
Whedon told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:22.680
"His fight work is wonderful: precise, heroic,&nbsp;
and you seldom have to double him. But one day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.040
he just turned wrong and his whole body&nbsp;
shut down... He was in enormous pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:29.179
and we had to shut that sequence down&nbsp;
and shoot it a couple of weeks later."

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:33.000
Ruby Rose sustained a spinal injury&nbsp;
while filming the first season of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.780
"Batwoman." The actor had surgery for two&nbsp;
herniated discs, sharing on Instagram:

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.280
"A couple of months ago I was told I needed&nbsp;
an emergency surgery or I was risking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.240
becoming paralyzed... I had herniated&nbsp;
two discs doing stunts, and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.500
close to severing my spinal cord. I was in&nbsp;
chronic pain and yet couldn't feel my arms."

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.660
After this injury, Rose completed filming season&nbsp;
one and departed the series. According to Vulture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:57.000
she later alleged that she&nbsp;
was pressured to return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.440
production only 10 days after surgery,&nbsp;
and that the CW production was unsafe.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.100
"Yeah, you know, it stings a&nbsp;
little. Just for reference."

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:10.080
Warner Bros. Television Group denied Rose's&nbsp;
allegations, and claimed the actor was let go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.400
due to colleagues' complaints. Notably, Rose&nbsp;
isn't the only actor who was hurt on set:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.540
A production assistant was left paralyzed&nbsp;
after being injured by a descending lift.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.500
We may never know the truth about the set&nbsp;
of "Batwoman," but we do know that Rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:26.658
required surgery for injuries sustained&nbsp;
while playing the titular character.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.480
While filming "Black Panther: Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever" in Boston, Letitia Wright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.220
who plays Shuri, suffered a concussion and a&nbsp;
shoulder fracture. Initially, no one expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.900
Wright's injuries to affect the production of the&nbsp;
blockbuster Marvel movie. But in November 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:44.520
production on the "Black Panther" sequel was&nbsp;
temporarily halted so Wright could recover.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.780
After Chadwick Boseman's tragic death,&nbsp;
Marvel decided not to recast the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.620
of T'Challa. This meant Shuri embarked on&nbsp;
a larger role in the sequel, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.700
taking on the mantle of the Black Panther&nbsp;
herself. Because of this increased profile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.540
the production needed to give Wright —&nbsp;
now the star — ample time to heal from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.960
her injuries. Like many movies filmed&nbsp;
during the pandemic, "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.100
Wakanda Forever" also experienced several&nbsp;
delays and setbacks, in addition to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.480
subject to ever-shifting regulations. This&nbsp;
forced everyone involved to be flexible.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.140
Marvel Studios executives sent&nbsp;
a note to the cast and crew of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:19.080
the production detailing the need&nbsp;
for a delay in filming. It read:

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.740
"Letitia had a frightening accident on&nbsp;
our set during a stunt back in August.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.280
It was a reminder of the importance&nbsp;
of safety at all times in our work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.460
which we know you understand and&nbsp;
are committed to. What we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.460
initially thought were minor injuries&nbsp;
turned out to be much more serious."

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:37.334
Happily, Wright was able to recover, and "Black&nbsp;
Panther: Wakanda Forever" made it to theaters.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.840
In very rare circumstances, something worse&nbsp;
than serious damage befalls actors. Tragically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.920
this is what happened to Brandon Lee&nbsp;
when he was filming "The Crow" in 1993.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.000
"The Crow" follows the titular hero, an&nbsp;
avenging angel with goth-rock swagger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.800
as he seeks revenge for his own death.

00:09:53.282 --> 00:10:01.688
"Suddenly, I heard a tapping, as of someone&nbsp;
gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door."

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.460
During production, Lee was shot with&nbsp;
a prop gun that accidentally contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.540
the tip of a dummy round in the barrel of&nbsp;
the firearm. He died about 12 hours later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.260
His death raised major concerns about&nbsp;
firearm safety in the film industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:17.100
and remains one of the saddest Hollywood&nbsp;
stories of the past few decades.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.500
District Attorney Jerry Spivey declined to press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.860
charges against the movie's production&nbsp;
company. As he told The New York Times:

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:26.220
"There's a part of me that wants to&nbsp;
file charges and have a trial. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.800
from a purely legal point of view,&nbsp;
I would not feel comfortable."

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:33.540
Lee's mother, Linda Lee Cadwell, filed a civil&nbsp;
suit and settled out of court. Paramount Pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.100
backed out of distributing the film because&nbsp;
of Lee's death, but it made $94 million at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.360
the box office and gained a cult following.&nbsp;
In an eerie example of life imitating art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.380
Lee, like his character, was young,&nbsp;
talented and on the cusp of stardom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:50.100
when his life was cut short. He will always&nbsp;
be remembered for his role in "The Crow."

